# Security and caching headers.
#
# This file's syntax is read natively by both Netlify and Cloudflare Pages, so
# choosing between them is not a decision this repo has to encode. See DEPLOY.md.
#
# BRIEF.md §9: "a CSP that permits only own origin (check 6 makes this
# achievable)". Check 6 is green, so it is achievable, and here it is. Nothing
# below is aspirational — tests/checks/14-deploy-posture.test.js parses this file
# and fails the build if a directive is missing or has been widened.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'none'; style-src 'self'; style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'none'; object-src 'none'; frame-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), autoplay=(), camera=(), display-capture=(), encrypted-media=(), fullscreen=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), midi=(), payment=(), publickey-credentials-get=(), screen-wake-lock=(), usb=(), xr-spatial-tracking=()
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
# Nothing here is meant to be read cross-origin. Detaching costs nothing if the
# host never sends it, and Cloudflare documents `! Header-Name` as the way to
# remove one. Check 15 has always asserted this header is absent on the wire and
# told the operator to remove it exactly this way; until now the line it named
# did not exist, and the parser could not have seen it if it had.
  ! Access-Control-Allow-Origin

# §9: "Long-lived immutable caching on version paths; short on current paths."
#
# ── Why /* declares no Cache-Control ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Cloudflare's documentation is explicit on two points that together make an
# overlapping declaration unsafe:
#
#   "An incoming request which matches multiple rules' URL patterns will inherit
#    ALL rules' headers."
#   "If a header is applied twice in the _headers file, the values are joined
#    with a comma separator."
#
# /oal/v1.0/styles.css matches both /oal/v1.0/* and /*. With a Cache-Control on
# each, the header on the wire would have been
#
#   public, max-age=600, must-revalidate, public, max-age=31536000, immutable
#
# — two max-age values and a must-revalidate in one header, on the one directory
# that is frozen forever. Worse, check 15 asserts /immutable/ *matches* there,
# and that string does match, so the check would have gone green over a broken
# freeze. Every path now matches exactly one Cache-Control declaration, so what
# is served can be read off this file rather than inferred from join order.
#
# Current paths therefore take Cloudflare's default, public, max-age=0,
# must-revalidate — shorter than the 600s declared here before, so §5's "short on
# current paths" still holds, and a correction still reaches a returning reader.
# Check 14 asserts the absence deliberately so it cannot be reinstated by accident.

# ── Cache-Control is stated BY TYPE, not by path ────────────────────────────
#
# Until 2026-08-12 this file carried one rule, `/oal/v1.0/*`, applying a year of
# `immutable` to everything under a published version — including the HTML
# document. That was already wrong and it was measured wrong:
#
#   /oal/v1.0/   200  public, max-age=31536000, immutable
#
# A path rule cannot tell a stylesheet from the page that links it. `immutable`
# on a *document* means a browser that has loaded it will not ask again for a
# year, and a purge cannot recall it — so a redesign, or a correction, reaches
# nobody who has already visited. The user's R3: no cache header may prevent a
# visitor from seeing the site as it is currently designed. Every page.
#
# So the rules below are stated by what a thing IS:
#
#   fingerprinted or frozen assets  →  immutable, one year. Correct and free:
#                                      their bytes cannot change under their URL.
#   everything else, HTML included  →  revalidate on the next request.
#
# Check 28 holds every rendered HTML document to this, reading the response
# rather than this file, and confirms revalidation with a 304 against the live
# host. Every path still matches exactly ONE Cache-Control declaration — see the
# comma-join note above; overlapping blocks concatenate rather than override.

# The frozen version's own bytes. These are the published rendering of a
# methodology document that scorecards cite: they can never legally change, so a
# year of immutable caching costs nothing and buys a scorecard reader in 2032 a
# page that loads from cache. This is part of how R2 holds.
#
# The DOCUMENT at /oal/v1.0/ is deliberately not listed. It matches no rule here
# and takes the host default, `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate` — the same as
# every other page — because its chrome now renders live and a stale copy would
# show a visitor last year's site.
/oal/v1.0/*.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/oal/v1.0/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/oal/v1.0/favicon.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The chrome stylesheet is derived and content-hashed — /chrome.<sha>.css — so a
# chrome change is a new URL rather than a new body at an old one. That is what
# lets it hold immutable under R3: there is no stale version to serve, because
# the redesigned page links a different file.
/chrome.*.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The live stylesheet is NOT fingerprinted — it is copied unhashed and
# unminified on purpose, because somebody will read it (§4) and a version
# snapshot has to stay reproducible. That makes an explicit rule necessary
# rather than optional: measured on 2026-08-12, with nothing declared here, the
# zone's own default served it as
#
#   /styles.css  200  public, max-age=14400, must-revalidate
#
# — four hours in which a redesign is invisible to a returning visitor, on every
# page on the site. The comment above about current paths taking
# `max-age=0, must-revalidate` was true of HTML and false of this file, and
# nothing in the repository had ever read the response to find out.
/styles.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# The live fonts are vendored and pinned but their filenames are not hashed, so
# they get a long cache rather than an immutable one: a deliberate font change
# propagates within a week instead of never. /oal/v1.0/fonts/ is not matched by
# this pattern and takes the immutable rule above, which is correct — those are
# the frozen copies.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

# script-src is 'none' above and there is no JavaScript on this site. If that
# ever stops being true, this comment is the thing to delete first, and the
# reason has to go in CHANGES.md.
